, {"t": " to", "lp": -0.8491026163101196, "top": [[" to", -0.8491026163101196], [" in", -1.4793212413787842]]}, {"t": " get", "lp": -0.5518280267715454, "top": [[" get", -0.5518280267715454], [" confirm", -0.8610875010490417]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.18617355823516846, "top": [[" the", -0.18617355823516846], [" authoritative", -2.67510724067688]]}, {"t": " precise", "lp": -0.7288020849227905, "top": [[" precise", -0.7288020849227905], [" authoritative", -1.1521705389022827]]}, {"t": ",", "lp": -0.4721336364746094, "top": [[",", -0.4721336364746094], [" semantics", -1.0744209289550781]]}, {"t": " version", "lp": -0.39075371623039246, "top": [[" version", -0.39075371623039246], [" current", -1.5864667892456055]]}, {"t": "-specific", "lp": -0.18840757012367249, "top": [["-specific", -0.18840757012367249], ["-app", -1.9129120111465454]]}, {"t": " semantics", "lp": -0.319290429353714, "top": [[" semantics", -0.319290429353714], [" behavior", -1.6064341068267822]]}, {"t": ".", "lp": -0.6746571063995361, "top": [[".", -0.6746571063995361], [" for", -0.769295871257782]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver can \"steal\" CCS contexts from other workloads when needed\n- More aggressive resource management\n- Better for mixed/multi-workload scenarios where you want the driver to dynamically allocate CCS resources\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static assignment**: CCS contexts are assigned more statically\n- Less dynamic stealing behavior\n- Generally more predictable but potentially less flexible under contention\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- Used when you want to **disable CCS stealing entirely**\n- Each context gets its dedicated CCS resources without the driver interfering\n- Useful for **performance-sensitive single-workload scenarios** where you want deterministic behavior\n\n---\n\n## When to use each\n\n| Mode | Best for |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | General use, multi-workload environments |\n| **2** | When you want more predictable CCS allocation |\n| **4** | Single heavy workload, performance tuning, avoiding stealing overhead |\n\n---\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f **Important caveat**: I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of these specific mode numbers, as the `ccs_mode` parameter and its exact values have evolved across different versions of the Xe driver. The behavior I described above is my best understanding, but:\n\n- The exact meaning of modes 1, 2, and 4 may differ between driver versions\n- These are relatively low-level tuning parameters\n\n**I'd recommend** checking your specific driver version's documentation or running:\n```bash\nmodinfo xe\n```\nor looking at the kernel source for the `ccs_mode` parameter definition to get the precise, version-specific semantics."}